EXTRA = [
    {"after": "readiness", "id": "disaster", "nav": "Disaster by hazard", "tag": "Clinical application",
     "h2": "Disasters by hazard: continuity plans, emergency kits, transfers, and records",
     "html": """
<p>The earlier sections described the cycle of readiness: before, during, and after an event, the roll call, and how patients are prioritized when slots are scarce. This section looks at the hazards one at a time, because each threatens a different link in the chain that keeps a patient on hemodialysis (HD) or peritoneal dialysis (PD) alive, and then at three tools that answer all of them: the unit continuity plan, the patient&rsquo;s emergency kit, and the transfer record. The patient-facing detail, including typhoon signal levels and the emergency diet, is in the site guide on typhoon and disaster preparedness for dialysis patients, which nurses can use as a teaching companion.</p>
<p><strong>The plain model.</strong> An HD treatment needs five things at once: a patient who can reach the unit, staff who can reach it too, electricity, treated water, and supplies. PD at home needs the patient, clean supplies, a clean place to do exchanges, and, for a cycler, electricity. A disaster is dangerous to a dialysis patient when it breaks any one of those links for longer than the patient can safely wait. Planning is the work of finding a second route for each link before it breaks.[[sever2023]]</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Hazard</th><th>Links it usually breaks</th><th>What the plan adds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Typhoon</td><td>Travel, power, and staff, usually with some warning</td><td>Sessions moved earlier by order; confirmed backup units outside the storm track; supplies and fuel topped up before landfall; staff call-in decided in advance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood</td><td>Travel and water quality; the building itself if the unit is on a low floor</td><td>Machines, records, and stock kept above the expected flood level where possible; patients in flood-prone areas identified early; no restart until the water system is tested (Module 8)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Earthquake</td><td>Everything at once, with no warning; the building and the water pipes may be damaged</td><td>Machines and shelves secured; a rehearsed procedure for patients on the machine during shaking; a building check before anyone returns; backup units in another area</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power outage</td><td>Machines, water treatment, lighting, and cold storage of medicines</td><td>Generator tested under load on a schedule, with fuel for the planned duration; which machines and water equipment run on backup power is known; manual blood return practiced</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water outage or contamination</td><td>Dialysate production; hand hygiene; cleaning</td><td>Stored water capacity known; an agreement on supply by tanker or from the utility; how long the unit can run on stored water calculated in advance</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>During an earthquake with patients on the machine.</strong> Nurses stay with their patients. The usual principle is to protect heads from falling objects, keep the patient in the chair with the lines secured, and not to start disconnecting while the floor is moving, because a needle pulled out during shaking can bleed heavily. When the shaking stops, the charge nurse decides whether to return blood and end treatments or to use the emergency disconnection method described earlier in this module series, and whether to evacuate. Each patient leaves with the access covered and secured, and the delivered treatment is recorded as soon as it is safe to do so.</p>
<p><strong>The unit continuity plan.</strong> The plan is a written document, approved by the medical director and kept on paper as well as electronically. It usually covers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hazard map:</strong> which hazards are likely for this building and this patient population, including which barangays or towns flood first.</li>
<li><strong>Utilities:</strong> generator capacity and fuel, stored water, and the contacts for the power and water utilities.</li>
<li><strong>Supplies:</strong> how many days of dialyzers, lines, concentrates, needles, and medicines the unit keeps on hand, and the supplier&rsquo;s emergency delivery arrangement.</li>
<li><strong>People:</strong> the staff call-in plan, how staff who cannot travel are covered, and arrangements for staff who must stay overnight.</li>
<li><strong>Partners:</strong> written arrangements with backup units in different areas, the receiving hospital for emergencies,[[doh2012]] and the local government disaster office. Nationally, disaster response is coordinated through the National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council, working with local government units.</li>
<li><strong>Communication:</strong> who speaks for the unit, a group message list for patients and staff, a fallback when mobile networks fail, and a posted notice at the unit entrance.</li>
<li><strong>Review:</strong> when the plan is reviewed, who signs it, and how changes are taught to staff.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Department of Health (DOH) licensing rules and the Philippine Society of Nephrology (PSN) guidelines set the requirements that apply to the facility;[[doh2012]][[psn2024]] the plan should be checked against the current versions of both.</p>
<p><strong>The patient emergency kit.</strong> The card or sheet described earlier is the core. Around it, each patient is helped to prepare a waterproof bag kept ready by the door, containing:</p>
<ul>
<li>the dialysis information card with the prescription, target weight, access type, recent serology results with dates, allergies, and contact numbers for the unit, the nephrologist, and at least one backup unit;</li>
<li>a list of medicines and a reserve supply, as the physician advises;</li>
<li>foods suited to the emergency diet and a measured cup for fluids, so that limits can be kept away from home;</li>
<li>a flashlight, a charged power bank, and some cash;</li>
<li>for a patient with diabetes, a glucose meter and a quick source of sugar;</li>
<li>for a patient on PD, a reserve of supplies as the PD team advises, alcohol hand rub, masks, clean dressings, and written instructions for manual exchanges in case the cycler cannot run.</li>
</ul>
<p>The emergency diet itself is taught in principle in the readiness section and in detail in the site guide; its amounts come from the nephrologist and dietitian.</p>
<p><strong>Patients on PD.</strong> PD can continue through many disasters if supplies and a clean space last, which is an advantage. The risks are different from HD: a cycler stops without power, so patients need to have practiced manual exchanges; floodwater and crowded evacuation centers make infection of the catheter exit site and of the abdomen more likely; and deliveries of solution bags may stop for days. Patients are taught to keep supplies off the floor, to do exchanges in the cleanest available space with hand hygiene, and to report cloudy fluid, abdominal pain, or fever at once.</p>
<p><strong>Transferring patients to other units.</strong> When patients must dialyze elsewhere, the receiving unit needs enough to treat them safely on the first session. The transfer record includes the current prescription and target weight, the date and details of the last treatment, the access type and any recent problems, serology results with dates, the most recent laboratory results, medicines and allergies, and the nephrologist&rsquo;s contact number. A patient who arrives at another unit with a hepatitis B status that cannot be confirmed may have to be treated as if positive until tested, which delays care; dated serology results on paper prevent this. The sending unit keeps a list of where each patient went, so that every patient can be brought back and followed up.</p>
<p><strong>Records.</strong> Electronic records may be unreachable when power or networks fail, and paper records can be destroyed by water. The unit therefore keeps an up-to-date summary list of all patients in more than one form and place, such as a printed copy in a waterproof container and an encrypted copy that a designated person can open off-site, following the facility&rsquo;s data-privacy rules. Treatments given during an emergency, including shortened sessions and treatments at other units, are entered in the record as soon as possible, with the reason for any change from the prescription.</p>
"""},
    {"after": "disaster", "id": "disaster-drill", "nav": "Disaster drill", "tag": "Quality improvement",
     "h2": "Running a disaster drill and learning from it",
     "html": """
<p>A plan that has never been tested is a hope. Drills show whether people know their parts, whether phone numbers still work, and whether equipment does what the plan assumes. They also give staff the experience of acting under pressure before it is real.</p>
<p><strong>Kinds of drill.</strong> Units usually build up from simple to complex:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tabletop exercise:</strong> the team sits together and talks through a scenario, such as a typhoon forecast to make landfall in two days, step by step, using the written plan. It is cheap and finds gaps in the plan itself.</li>
<li><strong>Functional drill:</strong> one part of the plan is performed for real, such as calling every patient on the contact tree within a set time, starting the generator and running the unit on it, or practicing manual blood return on a training circuit.</li>
<li><strong>Full drill:</strong> a whole shift practices a scenario, such as an earthquake during treatment followed by evacuation, with simulated patients or staff playing patients. Real patients are never placed at risk to make a drill realistic.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Roles.</strong> Each role is written down, assigned by position rather than by name so that it is filled on every shift, and briefed before the drill:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Responsibilities in the drill</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Person in charge (usually the charge nurse or the physician on duty)</td><td>Declares the emergency, decides whether to end treatments or evacuate, assigns tasks, and keeps a timeline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clinical team</td><td>Stays with assigned patients, returns blood or disconnects by the unit method, assesses each patient, and records the delivered treatment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Technical lead (dialysis technician)</td><td>Isolates and secures water and power equipment, starts the generator if used, and reports the state of the water system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Communication lead</td><td>Calls the medical director, backup units, and families; keeps the contact tree moving; posts notices</td></tr>
<tr><td>Records lead</td><td>Takes the printed patient list and the transfer records; checks that every patient and staff member is accounted for at the assembly point</td></tr>
<tr><td>Observers</td><td>Do not take part; note times, delays, and problems for the review</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Running it safely.</strong> The drill is announced to patients and staff where surprise is not the point of the exercise, and a clear phrase is agreed to stop it at once if a real emergency occurs. Equipment used for practice, such as a training circuit, is labeled so that it cannot reach a patient.</p>
<p><strong>The after-action review.</strong> Held as soon as possible after the drill, while memory is fresh, it is a short, blame-free discussion built on four questions:</p>
<ol>
<li>What did we plan to happen?</li>
<li>What actually happened? (The observers&rsquo; timeline helps here.)</li>
<li>Why was there a difference?</li>
<li>What will we change, who will do it, and by when?</li>
</ol>
<p>Typical findings include outdated phone numbers, a generator that started but could not carry the full load, a flashlight with dead batteries, staff unsure who should call the backup unit, and patients who could not find their information cards. Each action is written with a named owner and a date, and the next drill checks whether it was done. This links the drill to the improvement methods earlier in the module: a measure such as &ldquo;percentage of patients reached within the target time&rdquo; can be tracked from drill to drill like any other unit indicator. The plan, the drill dates, the attendance, and the review actions are kept on file, because a licensing inspection may ask for evidence that the plan is practiced.</p>
<p><strong>The nurse&rsquo;s part.</strong> Every nurse should be able to say, without looking, where the printed patient list is kept, how to return blood by hand on the unit&rsquo;s machines, where the emergency disconnection supplies are, which patients are on their branch of the contact tree, and where the assembly point is. A nurse who cannot answer one of these has found an item for the next review.</p>
"""},
]
